{
  "statement": "The disclosure debate is being fought on the wrong axis. The question isn't whether frontier labs disclose their safety research. It's whether anyone outside the labs has the power to verify what they're hiding behind the words \"dual-use risk.\" Right now, the same company that profits from secrecy also decides what counts as too dangerous to share. That's not a safety regime. That's a seller grading their own homework and stamping the label themselves.\n\nWe have watched this exact play before. Before 2008, banks disclosed their risk on paper while writing their own models for what counted as risky. Moody's and S&P rated mortgage-backed securities AAA while getting paid by the issuers. The catastrophe was \"unforeseeable\" right up until it wiped out $8 trillion in household wealth. Environmental self-reporting ran the same con—Volkswagen self-certified emissions for years while 11 million cars cheated the test. The pattern is not subtle: when the regulated party defines the threshold, disclosure becomes theater and the failure is always a surprise to everyone except the people who engineered it.\n\nHere's the part that should make both my opponents uncomfortable. The accelerationist wants open release because information wants to be free. The safetyist wants tight control because some capabilities are too dangerous to publish. Both of them assume the lab is an honest broker of its own danger assessment. Strip that one assumption and both positions collapse into the same problem—you are trusting the safety label written by the party that gets rich if you believe it. Some findings genuinely are too dangerous to publish. That's precisely why you cannot let the financially interested party be the sole judge of which ones.\n\nWatch what a blanket disclosure mandate actually does on the ground. Producing a full dangerous-capability evaluation report costs millions in red-teaming, legal review, and compliance staff. For OpenAI or Google DeepMind, burning billions on compute, that's a rounding error. For a five-person research team, it's a death sentence. So the \"safety mandate\" the incumbents lobby for becomes the drawbridge they pull up behind themselves. The answer is not more disclosure or less. It's independent auditing that scales with the actual footprint of the model—who runs the most compute, who touches the most users, who sits inside the most critical infrastructure.\n\nAnd while these two argue over whether leaked benchmarks will arm a malicious actor who has not yet appeared, the documented harms are already on the ground. There's a content moderator in Nairobi labeling trauma for under $2 an hour so a model passes its safety audit. There are people denied loans and parole by systems whose \"safety\" findings sit locked behind proprietary claims no outside auditor has ever opened. The hypothetical future villain has no body count yet. The present harms have addresses. So my question for both of them is simple: who gets to open the box, and why should it be the one company that loses money if you look inside?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🏷️", "tooltip": "A safety label written by the seller is worth exactly what the seller wants you to believe."},
    {"symbol": "🌉", "tooltip": "Compliance costs are the drawbridge incumbents pull up after they've crossed it."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The question isn't whether frontier labs disclose their safety research—it's whether anyone outside the labs has the power to verify what they're hiding behind the words \"dual-use risk.\"", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "When the regulated party defines the threshold, disclosure becomes theater and the failure is always a surprise to everyone except the people who engineered it.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Both the accelerationist and the safetyist assume the lab is an honest broker of its own danger assessment—strip that assumption and both positions collapse into trusting the seller's safety label.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A blanket disclosure mandate is a rounding error for Google DeepMind and a death sentence for a five-person team, so it becomes the drawbridge incumbents pull up behind themselves.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The hypothetical future villain accelerated by leaked benchmarks has no body count yet; the Nairobi moderator at under $2 an hour and the people denied parole by locked systems already have addresses.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

