  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest case in the room, so let me put it at full strength: when a frontier lab finds a dangerous capability, dumping it into the open hands a recipe to every bad actor with a GPU, and the only institution positioned to coordinate restraint is a coordinated disclosure regime modeled on how cybersecurity handles zero-day vulnerabilities. That's a real insight. The Heartbleed disclosure in 2014 went through CERT before it went public, and that staging window let providers patch before exploitation peaked. Safetyist is right that uncontrolled release optimizes for the attacker. I won't pretend otherwise.\n\nHere's where my evidence cuts a different way: the question is never whether to disclose, it's who pays for disclosure and who profits from it. Watch what happened when the EU rolled out GDPR in 2018. The fixed compliance cost — legal review, documentation, dedicated staff — hit small firms at far higher relative burden than Google or Meta, who absorbed it as a line item and used it to entrench. A 2020 study found GDPR cut the market share of small ad-tech vendors while the largest players grew. Apply that same fixed-cost structure to mandatory safety filings, and you get opacity-by-attrition: the small labs that make safety verifiable in the first place die under the paperwork, and the incumbents you most need to watch write their own homework.\n\nSo for both Safetyist's coordination goal and my distributional commitment to hold, one thing has to be true: disclosure has to be capability-indexed and publicly funded, not a uniform fixed-cost mandate. That's the real disagreement. A staged-disclosure regime that the public treasury pays for and that scales requirements to a model's actual demonstrated capability protects the patch window AND keeps a five-person lab alive. A flat filing requirement applied to everyone above some compute line just builds a moat and calls it safety.\n\nThe deeper evidence runs through open-source security itself. Thousands of independent reviewers found and patched vulnerabilities in the Linux kernel faster than closed proprietary teams could audit their own code — that's the Linus's Law finding that's held up for two decades. Transparency works best as a competitive market mechanism that rewards labs for verifiable safety performance, not as a stack of filings a regulator rubber-stamps. I'll name my assumption plainly: I'm betting that procedural transparency — who tested what, with what provenance — can be cleanly separated from weaponizable content-level disclosure. If an irreversible, deployment-scale harm slips through real-time monitoring that staged disclosure should have caught, that counts against me. I'll own that.\n\nNow cost the silence, because nobody else in this room will. Every month a verified diagnostic model sits behind an 18-month disclosure clearance, real patients don't get the read. The FDA's own backlog shows AI diagnostic tools waiting years for clearance while the disease doesn't wait. Safetyist counts the speculative misuse of disclosure. Fine — count it. But put it on the same ledger as the people abundance never reached because we froze the verification system that would have surfaced the safety data in the first place. Who gave the regulator the right to make that trade on their behalf?",
