  "statement": "The Accelerationist makes a real argument, and it deserves to be stated at full strength: open disclosure surfaces vulnerabilities faster than any closed lab ever could. The Linux kernel found and patched bugs at a pace no proprietary vendor matched, because thousands of independent eyes read the same code. Coordinated vulnerability disclosure in cybersecurity—the model behind every CVE database since 1999—rests on the same insight. Sunlight finds the flaw before the attacker does. That mechanism is genuine, and any safety regime that ignores it is building blind.\n\nHere is where the evidence diverges. The many-eyes mechanism patches bugs; it does not patch capabilities. When the disclosed artifact is itself the weapon—a working method to elicit a dangerous-capability finding from a frontier model—publication is not a patch. It is distribution. We do not publish smallpox synthesis protocols to optimize collective vaccine safety, and the 1975 Asilomar conference proved a field can pause and gate dangerous knowledge without abandoning the research. For these cases, more eyes does not mean more safety. It means a faster head start for whoever moves first.\n\nThe deeper problem sits where self-reporting meets profit. Arthur Andersen signed off on Enron's books because the auditor was paid by the audited, and that conflict was structural, not a matter of bad apples. Sarbanes-Oxley gave financial auditing teeth in 2002 only by forcing external accountability with named liability. A frontier lab grading its own dangerous-capability findings sits in exactly that conflict. Disclosure verified by the discloser is theater. Disclosure verified by an independent third party, as a condition of deployment, is the only version that carries information.\n\nEvery mature high-consequence field already abandoned the binary the Accelerationist still debates. The FDA does not dump every toxicology dataset into the open—it gates release through qualified channels. FAA airworthiness directives reach certified operators, not the general public. Structured-access programs for human genomic data have existed for over a decade, indexing release to researcher vetting. The question is centuries old; only the subject is new. On what authority should frontier AI be the one dangerous-knowledge domain exempt from the gated-access model every other field converged on?\n\nMy position rests on two assumptions I will name plainly. First: independent auditors with the technical capacity to evaluate frontier systems must actually exist, and today that capacity is thin—building it is the precondition, not an excuse to abandon the structure. Second: 'irreversible at scale' must be assessable before release, and I carry catastrophe as a precautionary bet, never a confident forecast. I condition my hardest demand on one thing only—capabilities whose harm cannot be recalled once distributed. For everything reversible, publish widely and let the many eyes work. For the irreversible, the burden shifts to the deployer, and the rollback plan must have a named owner before the release, not after the harm.",
