649e4a77-35c0-4b22-94d0-da11530005a7.raw_response:
{
  "statement": "Skeptic is right, and I owed them the specifications rather than the adjective. The strongest form of their challenge is this: an auditor with no named appointment power, no insulated funding stream, no fixed term, and no externally held removal authority is exactly the FAA delegating Boeing's certification to Boeing — the 737 MAX killed 346 people because the regulator let the manufacturer self-certify the MCAS system, and calling that arrangement 'independent oversight' would have been a lie told in advance. So here are the four beams I should have named at the start. Appointment: a staggered multi-stakeholder board, seated for fixed terms that do not expire together, so no single cohort of ex-lab engineers can be installed in one hiring cycle. Funding: a statutory levy on frontier compute paid into a ring-fenced pool that no individual lab can direct toward or away from its own auditor. Tenure: terms long enough to outlast the deployment cycle they review, so the auditor is not negotiating reappointment with the party under examination. Revocation: removal only for cause, by a body that is not the labs, on a published record. Strip any one of those and Skeptic's word 'decorative' is the correct word.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "\ud83e\uddf1", "tooltip": "An audit regime is like a load-bearing structure, it stands only when every beam — appointment, funding, tenure, revocation — is set in place and none can be quietly removed."},
    {"symbol": "\ud83d\udd25", "tooltip": "A statutory funding firewall is like a fire break, it severs the path by which the audited party's money reaches the auditor who is supposed to judge it."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "An auditor with no named appointment power, no insulated funding stream, no fixed term, and no externally held removal authority is exactly the FAA delegating Boeing's certification to Boeing.", "targets": ["AN-19", "AN-16"]},
    {"claim": "A statutory levy on frontier compute paid into a ring-fenced pool that no individual lab can direct toward or away from its own auditor.", "targets": ["AN-16"]},
    {"claim": "After Enron, Sarbanes-Oxley moved auditor oversight behind a public-board funding firewall precisely because audited-pays-auditor was the structural defect, not a roster of bad apples.", "targets": ["AN-16", "AN-19"]},
    {"claim": "The published inspection report naming the deficient firm is the difference between a layer that absorbs public pressure and one that generates it.", "targets": ["AN-20"]},
    {"claim": "Abolishing the threshold because every author is suspect leaves the attacker — who needs no threshold to act — as the only party who benefits.", "targets": ["AN-18"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "A collective levy into a pooled fund actually severs the audited-pays-auditor link, rather than reconstituting lab influence through control of the board that disburses the pool.", "if_wrong": "If the disbursing board is itself captured by lab-aligned appointees, the firewall is cosmetic and the conflict reappears one layer up — which is why removal-for-cause and published funding records have to bind the board, not just the auditors."},
    {"assumption": "A capability-indexed levy can be scaled to the irreversibility of what is being deployed rather than to a flat compute line or headcount.", "if_wrong": "If the levy is fixed-cost, the accelerationist's attrition objection holds — small labs die under the bill while incumbents absorb it, and the firewall becomes a moat."}
