tly by ex-lab engineers, so cultural capture at the floor-writing layer is real. The defense is provenance, not purity — a floor fixed in statute that no lab can subtract from is gameable only by what it omits, never by what it quietly removes, and an omission is visible to the next evaluator in a way a silent deletion is not.\n\nThe compute levy maps to a working precedent, and I want to be precise about where the map ends. The Public Company Accounting Oversight Board is funded by mandatory support fees levied on issuers in proportion to market capitalization — a pool no single issuer directs toward or away from its own auditor. That is the same severance of payer from appointer that sank Arthur Andersen, where the auditor was paid by the audited. The levy inherits that structure cleanly. Where the analogy breaks is the fifth beam itself: a securities auditor reads a fixed, frozen ledger, while a frontier auditor must read a moving artifact behind an access gate the audited party controls. There is no PCAOB precedent for compelled raw access to a living technical system, which is exactly why this beam cannot be borrowed — it must be drafted new, in statute, with the access terms named on the page.\n\nNone of this binds if a lab can simply route around an adverse finding. An independent auditor's entire value is that it will sometimes say no, and a lab facing a 'no' has an obvious move: deploy anyway and absorb the reputational cost. So deployment above the frontier compute threshold without a current clearance cannot be treated as a risk to weigh on a ledger — it must be a legally void act, with strict liability attaching to the named officers who authorize it. An unaudited annual filing is not a risky filing; it is not a filing at all, and the company cannot list on its strength. Frontier deployment without a clearance must carry the same nullity. I hold the gate narrow on purpose — it attaches only above the compute threshold and only to capabilities that cannot