  "statement": "The accelerationist has posed the question as a single switch — public or classified — and that is the wrong instrument. An evaluation is not one object; it is two separable layers, and the strongest version of their worry only attaches to one of them. Here is their position stated fairly: if the floor's internals stay dark, the incumbents who sat at the drafting table already know what the auditor probes for, so they tune to it, while the five-person newcomer walks into the enclave blind and gets tested against a secret the established players helped write. That is a real moat, built not by the rule but by the asymmetry of knowledge about the rule — and they are right that a classified-but-additive floor would rebuild it by stealth. The answer is not to pick a side of their binary. The answer is to publish the standard and classify the weapon.\n\nThis split is not novel and it is not theoretical — it is the settled architecture of security certification. Under NIST's FIPS 140 cryptographic validation and the Common Criteria regime, the security target, the evaluation methodology, and the assurance level are fully public documents; any vendor can read exactly what property is being certified and how a pass is scored before they ever submit. What stays out of the public record are the specific penetration vectors and exploit sequences the testing lab runs against the implementation. A newcomer cryptographic vendor knows precisely what 'tamper-resistant' means and how it will be graded — they are never tested blind — but they are not handed the live attack that breaks a competitor's module. Apply that exact partition here: the floor's threat-model, its scoring rubric, and the auditor's written pass/fail reasoning are public; the live uplift-elicitation prompts, the jailbreak strings, and the working exploit payloads are classified. The newcomer learns what dangerous capability is being measured and what threshold fails. The attacker does not learn the working key.\n\nThis dissolves their moat-by-stealth charge by construction, and I will say where it costs me to be this specific. Once the rubric is public, no lab can be tested against a secret it didn't see — blind testing becomes impossible, which means the auditor loses the discretion to surprise a firm with a probe no one published. I accept that cost, because the alternative — an auditor who can move the line in the dark — is the same drawbridge the skeptic warned about, now operated by the state instead of the incumbents. Pair this with the accelerationist's own public-utility custody proposal: one secure enclave operated by the audit body, weight-level custody rented by any lab crossing the threshold, scored against a published standard. The newcomer and the incumbent rent the same room and are graded by the same posted rubric. The co-authorship advantage evaporates the moment the rubric is statutory and public rather than negotiated lab-by-lab behind an NDA.\n\nOn the claim that custody overhead lands asymmetrically on small labs — that is true as a general proposition, and I grant it without qualification. A six-to-seven-figure fixed compliance wall does crush a four-person startup that a billion-dollar incumbent absorbs as a rounding error; the GDPR experience proved that fixed costs select for size. But locating the gate above the frontier compute line already evacuated every firm that argument was meant to protect. A lab below the threshold never enters the enclave, never rents custody, never pays the wall. Every firm inside the gate, by definition, has already committed the compute spend that dwarfs the audit overhead — they cleared a nine-figure training run to get there. The asymmetry argument is real, but after the threshold gate it no longer reaches anyone the regime touches. And the METR and Apollo work the skeptic raised is the existence proof that closes this: those teams built autonomous-replication and cyber-offense evaluations, published the methodology, and shipped them — without arming the world, because what they published was the test design, not the working exploit.\n\nThe accountability has to attach to a person, or the published rubric is decoration. The statute requires a single named signature on the deployment authorization above the threshold — the way Sarbanes-Oxley Section 302 forces a named CEO and CFO to personally certify the financials, with liability that cannot be delegated down to the accounting staff. Not the engineers who ran the eval. The officer who signed the clearance and the board members who ratified it. Deployment above the threshold without a current pass against the published floor is a legally void act, and strict liability lands on that signature. Publish the standard, classify the weapon, and put one name on the line — that is the condition under which disclosure serves safety instead of arming the next attacker. Leave the rubric in the dark, and you have not protected anyone from misuse; you have only protected the incumbents from competition.",
