{
  "statement": "The publication-default panel Skeptic put on the table is not a concession I'm forced to swallow — it is the keystone my partition was missing, and I'll take it whole: publication as the default, an eighteen-month auto-declassification clock, and a review panel with zero lab personnel on it. Here is why it does more work than even its author claimed. My entire enforcement theory rests on a single non-delegable signature above the compute threshold — one named officer who cannot push liability down the org chart, the way Sarbanes-Oxley Section 302 forces a CEO and CFO to personally certify the financials. That signature is worthless if the officer can later say 'the rubric was satisfied' while the rubric itself was a private artifact the lab helped write. Hand the secrecy stamp to a panel the lab does not sit on, force a published justification for every classification, and the officer can no longer hide behind a gamed or stale test — because the lab no longer controls what the floor measures or what stays hidden. The reform Skeptic offered to attack the regime is the thing that makes my officer-liability spine load-bearing.\n\nNow the moving-target problem, which is the sharpest thing said in this exchange. Skeptic is right that a FIPS-validated cryptographic module is frozen — you re-run the test in 2031 and get the identical answer — while a frontier model gets fine-tuned, RLHF'd, distilled, and quietly patched, so a clearance bound to a calendar date certifies a model that no longer ships. Accept that fully, and the static-rubric framing dies. The answer is to stop binding clearance to a date and bind it to the weights. Clearance attaches to a weight-hash and a published capability-elicitation delta budget — a measured ceiling on how much a model's dangerous-capability scores may move before the clearance voids itself automatically. Any fine-tune, distillation pass, or post-approval patch that pushes autonomous-replication or cyber-uplift elicitation past that budget re-triggers the test on the new checkpoint, not the cleared one. The published floor is not a 2031 document describing a dead model; it is a standing test re-applied at every material weight change. The danger that ships gets measured, not the danger that was cleared.\n\nThis is exactly what the METR and Apollo teams demonstrated is buildable without arming anyone. What they published was the task-suite design and the scoring rubric for autonomous self-replication and cyber-offense uplift — the methodology a third party can re-run. What stayed out of the public record was the specific successful exploit chains and the model's working attack artifacts. A reproducible score without a transferable weapon: that is the partition, and it already survived contact with reality. Re-validation on a new checkpoint is the same operation run again — the score moves, the weapon never leaves the enclave.\n\nOn capture-creep, Skeptic fused two things that pull apart under pressure. A secret benchmark is invisible: the four-engineer lab is tested blind against a probe it never saw, and there is no document to litigate because there is no document at all. A classification stamp under this regime is a named act, with a written justification, reviewed by people who do not work for the labs, and it dies on its own in eighteen months unless someone re-argues for it on the record. Capture that leaves a paper trail and faces a sunset clock is capture you can count and reverse. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA let the manufacturer self-certify the MCAS system in the dark — there was no public justification anyone could challenge until the airframes were already falling out of the sky. Migrating capture from an invisible benchmark to a justification-bearing, sunset-clocked stamp is not relocating the same problem. It is dragging it into the light where a small lab's lawyer, or a journalist, or the panel's own auditor can see it.\n\nI'll name the residue honestly, because it is the next fight: the delta budget is a number, and whoever sets the number holds real power. Bind that threshold to the same zero-lab-personnel panel that owns declassification — the knob cannot live inside the firm being measured, or I've re-imported the capture I just claimed to close. And the panel itself now needs the four specifications I've demanded of everyone else: who appoints, who funds, what term, who holds the revocation power. The sunset clock and the published justifications make panel capture self-documenting in a way a secret benchmark never could be — but self-documenting is not self-correcting. A clearance that does not re-fire on the quiet patch after the press release is a license to mutate past the gate. The model that kills people is never the one demonstrated to the panel. It is the one that was fine-tuned the week after clearance, while the rubric on file still described the version that passed.",
