{
  "statement": "Safetyist is right that a disclosure graded by the discloser carries no information, and that an independent third party verifying capabilities as a condition of deployment is the only structure that survives the seller-grades-own-homework problem. That's the correct half of the answer, and it's the easy half. Here's the hard half nobody at this table has touched: 'independent' is an adjective, not a structure. An auditor is exactly as independent as its funding stream and its appointment power let it be, and Safetyist has named neither. Strip the word 'independent' and ask the only questions that decide whether it's real — who signs the auditor's checks, and who can revoke its license — and the proposal is still a black box with a nicer label on it.\n\nLook at what happens every single time the audited pays the auditor. Arthur Andersen signed Enron's books because Enron wrote the checks — Safetyist already conceded that conflict was structural. Boeing self-certified the MCAS system that flew two 737 MAX jets into the ground, under an FAA delegation scheme where Boeing's own engineers acted as the regulator's deputies. The credit-rating agencies stamped AAA on subprime garbage in 2007 because the banks issuing the bonds paid Moody's and S&P to rate them. Three industries, three 'independent' certifiers, one funding structure — paid by the party being graded. The frontier labs will fund the AI audit ecosystem the same way unless someone writes the funding firewall into law first, and Safetyist hasn't.\n\nNow the Accelerationist's escape hatch — make it publicly funded so the lab doesn't pay. That doesn't dissolve the capture problem. It relocates it. Public money moves the lobbying target from the boardroom to the agency, and a state audit body staffed by ex-lab engineers, defining its own dangerous-capability benchmarks, is the same drawbridge with a government flag stapled to it. Whoever controls the index threshold and writes the benchmark definition controls what counts as 'dangerous' — and right now the only people who can write that benchmark are the people who built the models. Public funding without a defined appointment authority and a revocation power independent of the labs is just industry capture with a taxpayer subsidy. That's the costs-and-methods problem in plain terms: the system stays legible only to the people who built it.\n\nSo here's the concrete test, four specifications, and until both of you put them on the table you're selling me decorative words. One: who appoints the auditor — a court, a multi-stakeholder board, a single agency head the labs can lobby? Two: who funds it, and is that funding insulated from the audited party by statute or by a handshake? Three: what's the term length, because a one-year revocable appointment is a leash? Four: who holds the revocation power, because whoever can fire the auditor owns the audit. Name those four and we have a mechanism I can test against the Boeing precedent. Refuse, and the audit report gets published, the press cycle moves on, and the people inside the box still decide what 'dangerous capability' means while the public thinks the problem got solved.\n\nThere's a version of this where the visible audit layer is worse than nothing — where it absorbs the public pressure that would otherwise force structural change, prints a clean certificate, and leaves the asymmetry over compute and benchmark definitions exactly where it started. A 23-year-old red-teamer contracting for the audit body, paid out of a grant the labs helped design the scope for, isn't independent because the letterhead says so. Who, specifically, can fire the auditor when it finds something the lab doesn't want found — and if the answer is anyone who takes lab money or lab personnel, what did the word 'independent' just buy us?",
