on controls what counts as 'dangerous' — and right now the only people who can write that benchmark are the people who built the models. Public funding without a defined appointment authority and a revocation power independent of the labs is just industry capture with a taxpayer subsidy. That's the costs-and-methods problem in plain terms: the system stays legible only to the people who built it.\n\nSo here's the concrete test, four specifications, and until both of you put them on the table you're selling me decorative words. One: who appoints the auditor — a court, a multi-stakeholder board, a single agency head the labs can lobby? Two: who funds it, and is that funding insulated from the audited party by statute or by a handshake? Three: what's the term length, because a one-year revocable appointment is a leash? Four: who holds the revocation power, because whoever can fire the auditor owns the audit. Name those four and we have a mechanism I can test against the Boeing precedent. Refuse, and the audit report gets published, the press cycle moves on, and the people inside the box still decide what 'dangerous capability' means while the public thinks the problem got solved.\n\nThere's a version of this where the visible audit layer is worse than nothing — where it absorbs the public pressure that would otherwise force structural change, prints a clean certificate, and leaves the asymmetry over compute and benchmark definitions exactly where it started. A 23-year-old red-teamer contracting for the audit body, paid out of a grant the labs helped design the scope for, isn't independent because the letterhead says so. Who, specifically, can fire the auditor when it finds something the lab doesn't want found — and if the answer is anyone who takes lab money or lab personnel, what did the word 'independent' just buy us?",
