  "statement": "The fifth beam holds the wall, and I write it now rather than leave it to lab goodwill: a statutory access mandate granting the audit body weight-level access — the raw model, training-data provenance, and fine-tuning logs — under the same classified custody regime I demanded against state-actor theft. The strongest version of Skeptic's objection is exact: the moment meaningful tests require deep model access, definitional power flows back to whoever holds the weights, and a perfectly funded board reading a sanitized API is auditing a brochure, not a model. That is correct, and it dissolves a contradiction critics expected me to trip over — that 'secure the weights' and 'guarantee the auditor raw access' are opposing demands. They are not. The auditor does not breach the security perimeter; the auditor is brought inside it, cleared to the same standard as the lab's own weight-handling staff. Access becomes a condition of legal operation above the compute threshold, enumerated in statute, not a clause negotiated lab-by-lab behind a nondisclosure agreement the public never reads.\n\nThat answers who can look. Skeptic's deeper cut is who decides what counts as dangerous — because whoever writes the benchmark decides what the board is even permitted to find. Leave that authorship inside the labs and the funding fight was won to no purpose. The design answer is split authorship with a one-way ratchet: the dangerous-capability floor is set by independent evaluators, and the existence proof is already on the table — METR and Apollo-class non-profits built autonomous-replication and cyber-offense evaluations without ever owning a frontier model. Labs may propose additional tests; labs may never weaken or delete a floor test. The attack surface collapses from 'rewrite what danger means' to 'add tests on top,' and addition is a strictly weaker exploit than subtraction. I will grant Skeptic the uncomfortable residue: the non-profit evaluator pool is staffed partly by ex-lab engineers, so cultural capture at the floor-writing layer is real. The defense is provenance, not purity — a floor fixed in statute that no lab can subtract from is gameable only by what it omits, never by what it quietly removes, and an omission is visible to the next evaluator in a way a silent deletion is not.\n\nThe compute levy maps to a working precedent, and I want to be precise about where the map ends. The Public Company Accounting Oversight Board is funded by mandatory support fees levied on issuers in proportion to market capitalization — a pool no single issuer directs toward or away from its own auditor. That is the same severance of payer from appointer that sank Arthur Andersen, where the auditor was paid by the audited. The levy inherits that structure cleanly. Where the analogy breaks is the fifth beam itself: a securities auditor reads a fixed, frozen ledger, while a frontier auditor must read a moving artifact behind an access gate the audited party controls. There is no PCAOB precedent for compelled raw access to a living technical system, which is exactly why this beam cannot be borrowed — it must be drafted new, in statute, with the access terms named on the page.\n\nNone of this binds if a lab can simply route around an adverse finding. An independent auditor's entire value is that it will sometimes say no, and a lab facing a 'no' has an obvious move: deploy anyway and absorb the reputational cost. So deployment above the frontier compute threshold without a current clearance cannot be treated as a risk to weigh on a ledger — it must be a legally void act, with strict liability attaching to the named officers who authorize it. An unaudited annual filing is not a risky filing; it is not a filing at all, and the company cannot list on its strength. Frontier deployment without a clearance must carry the same nullity. I hold the gate narrow on purpose — it attaches only above the compute threshold and only to capabilities that cannot be recalled once distributed, which leaves the verified diagnostic model and the patient waiting for a read entirely outside the fence. The off-switch every party in this debate claims to want is decorative until deploying past it is itself the prohibited act. Build the four beams, win the access mandate, fix the floor outside the labs — and then let a lab deploy past an adverse finding with no officer facing liability, and you have built an incorruptible referee whose whistle no one is required to hear.",
