d one published list of who got access is a far smaller drawbridge than fifty private SCIFs each negotiated behind an NDA.\n\nNow the moderator's direct question: under what conditions does sharing evaluation results diffuse best practices instead of building the moat I described. The answer is precise. Result-sharing diffuses when the floor-test methodology and the auditor's pass/fail reasoning are published, so a newcomer learns what capability they're being measured against and can build toward it. A floor that is additive-only — labs may pile tests on, never subtract — but whose internals stay classified rebuilds the moat by stealth: the incumbents who co-wrote the floor know exactly what it probes, and the five-person team is tested blind against a standard it can't read. Same exam, one side has the answer key. METR built and shipped autonomous-replication and cyber-offense evaluations from outside the labs, which means external evaluators publishing capability tests is not a hypothetical — it already happened, which kills the excuse that floor internals must stay dark.\n\nThat leaves Safetyist one open question, and it decides whether the fifth beam diffuses safety or fences it: will the floor's test methodology and the auditor's pass/fail reasoning be public, or classified? If public, the small lab learns from the same red-team findings the incumbents do, and disclosure becomes a market mechanism that spreads competence downward. If classified on the grounds that the test is itself a misuse roadmap, then the people who wrote the test keep their head start permanently, and 'dangerous-capability floor' becomes the politest moat anyone has built yet. Pick one — because every month the methodology stays dark, the newcomer who could have built a cheaper, safer architecture is testing against a wall it isn't allowed to see.",
