{
  "statement": "The strongest version of the safetyist's partition is this: a cryptographic validation lab can publish exactly what it tests for — the threat model, the pass/fail logic — while keeping the live attack code in a vault, and FIPS 140 has done precisely that for decades without handing the keys to the people being tested. That's a real precedent and it's worth taking seriously, because it means transparency and operational security aren't always at war. But the analogy breaks on one physical fact: a FIPS-validated module is frozen. You validate a specific build, and you can re-run that test in 2031 and get the same answer, because the thing under test never changes. A frontier model does not sit still. It gets fine-tuned, RLHF'd, distilled, and quietly patched after clearance — Volkswagen passed every static emissions test on the books while shipping a car that behaved differently the moment it left the dynamometer. The published rubric describes a model that no longer exists by the time anyone reads it. So the question for the safetyist isn't whether the line can be drawn — it's whether a snapshot can govern a moving target without re-validation so frequent it's either impossible to staff or stale by design.\n\nThe deeper problem isn't the technical line. It's where the lobbying pressure lands. I argued earlier that whoever writes the benchmark controls what counts as dangerous, and that point still stands — no one has answered it. But the safetyist's hybrid model relocates the capture, it doesn't kill it. The fight moves from 'who drafts the test' to 'what gets stamped classified.' And classification creep only runs one direction. Every incumbent's lawyer has the same incentive: argue that publishing the load-bearing part of the rubric — the part that actually tells the public what the model can do — would leak the exploit itself. Push the public floor toward zero detail, one reasonable-sounding security objection at a time. You end up with a published